


@@BEGIN: PRESENTATION
@id: dahlig-turek-matuszewska
@title: The Folk Ballad as a Digital Corpus: A Comparison of Materials from Oskar Kolberg’s Opera Omnia and the Essen Folksong Collection
@presenter: dahlig-turek; matuszewska
@abstract: 

	This paper presents a comparative analysis of two corpora
	of folk ballads: the Polish material from Oskar Kolberg’s
	collection and the German-language material from the <i>Essen
	Folksong Collection</i>, based, among other sources, on the
	edition <i>Deutsche Volkslieder mit ihren Melodien. Balladen</i>.
	Comparative research is central to digital musicology, as
	it makes it possible to move beyond the analysis of
	individual sources and to identify broader patterns within
	large repertorial collections. Despite the growing
	abundance of digital material, however, identifying
	suitable corpora for comparative research remains a
	significant challenge. Collections often differ in
	chronological and geographical scope, editorial principles,
	degree of completeness, or repertorial coverage, all of
	which complicate the reliable comparison of results. In the
	present project, the situation is particularly favourable:
	the two corpora are comparable in size and contain the same
	musical genre — the folk ballad — making it possible to
	undertake a statistical analysis aimed at identifying
	trends, similarities, and differences between the Polish
	and German-language materials.

	The Polish part of the study will be based on Oskar
	Kolberg’s collection, which contains the texts and melodies
	of 41 ballad types, transmitted in numerous variants from
	different regions of Poland, amounting to 444 ballads in
	total. The comparative material will consist of the
	German-language ballad corpus known as the <i>Essen Folksong
	Collection</i>, based, among other sources, on the eight-volume
	edition <i>Deutsche Volkslieder mit ihren Melodien. Balladen</i>,
	published by the Deutsches Volksliederarchiv between 1935
	and 1988. Although this corpus has long circulated within
	digital musicology, it has not yet been fully used as an
	object of musicological inquiry into the ballad repertory.
	More often, it has served as an accessible dataset for
	testing methods of symbolic music analysis rather than as
	material interpreted in accordance with one of its primary
	purposes: the documentation and analysis of the
	German-language song tradition. Only when placed alongside
	the digitised material of Oskar Kolberg does its potential
	become available in a way closer to its original source
	context — as a comparative corpus for the study of the
	structure, variability, and circulation of ballad melodies.

	The research will be conducted using files in the <b>EsAC</b>
	format, since this format is available for both corpora and
	enables work on comparable material encoded in symbolic
	form.

@@END: PRESENTATION



